WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our next guest is
a Grammy-nominated
comedian and actor.

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.471
He'll be appearing live
at Radio City Music Hall

00:00:05.471 --> 00:00:09.208
September 23rd to the 25th
as part of his "Fun Tour."

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:10.643
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:12.512
our very good friend
Jim Gaffigan.

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:14.580
♪♪
[ Applause ]

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.782
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:18.251
Oh, my God.
-You look nice.

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:20.252
-Wow, you guys!
-Yeah, this is really nice.

00:00:20.252 --> 00:00:22.555
-Thank you so much.
Thank you.

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.023
-Oh, don't milk it.
-Thank you.

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:25.858
Thanks so much. Thank you.

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:28.193
-They get tired
if they have to keep clapping.

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:29.495
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:32.098
No, I am not going to
make you -- Thanks so much.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.900
-Welcome.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.503
-Wow.
-Well, this will be --

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:39.104
Radio City won't be a big deal
after you came out to this.

00:00:39.104 --> 00:00:42.508
-Well, it's going to be weird
going back to Radio City.

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:45.511
Because, as you know, I was
a Rockette for a while.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:46.779
-You were?
-Yeah.

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:49.315
-I don't think I did know that.
-I was fired --

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.750
You know, I was part of
the Christmas spectacular.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.353
I don't know if you brought
your kids there.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:56.088
And I was fired.

00:00:56.088 --> 00:00:59.459
They caught me in a situation
with one of the donkeys.

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:00.693
Nothing --
-Okay.

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.428
-Nothing was going on.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:05.164
-I mean, the donkey was
definitely flirting with me.

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:07.366
-Right.
-But --

00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:09.836
-But that's a fireable offense
as a Rockette.

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:11.871
-Essentially, I got
a donkey pregnant.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:13.006
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:15.341
But it's -- you know,
it's, like, fine.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.943
-Wait. Nothing happened?
-My wife doesn't know.

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:19.245
-Oh, okay.
-So don't bring it up to her.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:20.713
She'd freak out.
-She doesn't watch the show.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.581
-She'd freak out.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:22.581 --> 00:01:23.950
So you played MSG as well.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.218
Those are kind of the big rooms
in the city.

00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:27.353
It must be thrilling.

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:28.988
-You know, I'm so excited.

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:31.157
And, you know, when I did
Madison Square Garden --

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:34.394
'cause my kids are going to come
and see me at Radio City.

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:35.695
And I was like,
"Well, you remember

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:37.529
when I was
at Madison Square Garden."

00:01:37.529 --> 00:01:38.765
And they were like, "No."

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:40.666
And it wasn't even
that long ago.

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:43.503
It's like kids remember nothing
'cause they don't care.

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:44.971
-Yeah.
-They don't care.

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:49.475
Because children are just God's
punishment for having sex.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:52.078
[ Laughter ]
I know that sounds hard.

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:54.380
-You guys have had a lot of sex,
'cause you have a lot of kids.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.249
-Five times we've had sex.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.017
You have five kids.
-Five kids.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:00.353
-Last time we talked
was April of 2020.

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:02.154
How did you hold up?
How did you make it through?

00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:04.523
-You know, the pandemic --
I actually --

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:06.759
Well, I died in June,
which was weird.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:07.827
-[ Laughs ]
-Okay.

00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:10.295
-We can cut that out.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.295 --> 00:02:12.565
-You did look a little --

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:14.900
-I mean, I looked a little pale.
-You've lost some color. Yeah.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.637
-I mean, it was crazy.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.540
You know, this is going to
sound pandering,

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.310
but I feel as though the
pandemic was hardest on mothers.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.145
-Mm-hmm.
-Definitely mothers, right?

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:28.347
Or the men who had to
live with those mothers.

00:02:28.347 --> 00:02:31.650
[ Laughter ]
But it was insane for everyone.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.555
There was, at one point, I --
I turned to my wife --

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:38.424
I guess it was after I finished
the first case of scotch.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.460
And I turned to her.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.695
And I said,
"When this is all over,

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.930
I will take you wherever
you want to go.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.232
Wherever you want to go."

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.601
And my wife was like,
"I want to go to Hawaii."

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.970
And I was like, "Well, I was
thinking more tri-state area."

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.038
[ Laughter ]
You know what I mean?

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:53.339
-Sure.
-She was like,

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:55.041
"I want to go to Hawaii,
and I want to bring the kids."

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:57.076
And I was like, "Whose kids?"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.811
And she was like, "Our kids."

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.512
And so I did research,

00:03:00.512 --> 00:03:02.949
and it was cheaper than
a divorce, so we went to Hawaii.

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:06.152
[ Laughter ]

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:10.089
-Uh-huh.
-And what an amazing place.

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.091
Right? It's like no other state.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.160
It's just --
It's a tropical paradise.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.865
It's almost like Hawaii was
this Polynesian island nation

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:20.199
that we just took over.

00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:21.701
Do you know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.969
Like, it wasn't even part
of the United States.

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:28.006
We just went in kind of like
it was, you know, an open house.

00:03:28.006 --> 00:03:30.409
We just looked around like,
"Oh, this is nice.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:32.712
We can have our wedding here."
You know what I mean?

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:34.847
And the Hawaiians were like,
"We're our own country."

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.115
And we're like,
"You're coming with us."

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:38.350
[ Laughter ]
And if you've been there,

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.653
you know that --
you know, understandably,

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.923
there are people that are angry.
-Yes. Justifiably so, yes.

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:45.257
-They'll come up to you
and they'll say,

00:03:45.257 --> 00:03:46.925
"You stole this land."

00:03:46.925 --> 00:03:48.661
And I'm like, "I don't know
how to break it to you,

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.496
but we stole all the land."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:51.597
You know?

00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:52.798
It probably just stings
a little more

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.199
'cause this land is prettier,

00:03:54.199 --> 00:03:57.069
but there's even people
in Detroit mad at us.

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:59.271
[ Laughter ]
You know?

00:03:59.271 --> 00:04:02.341
-Now, I would imagine --
I don't like the beach.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:03.609
I would imagine you don't.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:06.245
-No, no, no. I do not.

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:08.247
I know I look like I --

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:10.616
I just spent --
'Cause my wife loves the beach.

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:14.620
And so I just spent the entire
vacation just applying

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.891
and reapplying sunscreen, which
thankfully makes my skin whiter.

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.225
-Right.
-You know.

00:04:20.225 --> 00:04:24.763
I don't know what cruel bastard
decided that the goo

00:04:24.763 --> 00:04:26.698
that protects pale people
from the sun

00:04:26.698 --> 00:04:28.567
should also make them look like
white walkers.

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:29.635
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:31.103
-Do you know what I mean?

00:04:31.103 --> 00:04:33.572
I just didn't want to be the --
I didn't want to get burned.

00:04:33.572 --> 00:04:35.541
You don't want to be
the sunburned adult.

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:38.010
'Cause, you know, we've all --
You know, you see a kid

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.812
with a sunburn, and you're like,
"Parenting's hard."

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.447
But you see an adult
that's sunburnt

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:42.715
and you're like,
"That's an alcoholic."

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:43.816
[ Laughter ]
-Right.

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:46.652
-Right?
You need to avoid that person.

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:50.589
Like, sunburned adults always
look a little lost, right?

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:52.091
'Cause they're looking
for the bar.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:53.292
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:54.593
-That's why they look lost.

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:55.828
Did you do activities?

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:57.396
What activities did you do
when you were there?

00:04:57.396 --> 00:05:01.567
-You know, I don't know why
all the vacation activities

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.603
require a waiver,
you know what I mean?

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:04.803
-They do now.
-On vacations. Right?

00:05:04.803 --> 00:05:06.139
-I feel like that's a new thing.
Yeah.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:08.073
-Yeah. It's like,
"Sign here. Have fun.

00:05:08.073 --> 00:05:10.542
Hopefully you don't die." Right?
-Yeah.

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:13.813
-And we did zip lining
for the last time.

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:16.049
That's just --
-Did you not like it?

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:18.050
-What is wrong with humans?

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:22.054
It's like, "Oh, do you want to
feel like you're about to die?

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:25.825
Would you pay $200 to feel like
you're about to die six times?"

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:28.761
It's like why don't you just,
you know --

00:05:28.761 --> 00:05:31.764
Why don't you just go to a biker
rally and take off your mask?

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:35.635
You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:38.171
Why don't you walk into traffic?

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.772
[ Laughter ]
I don't know.

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:42.975
But sometimes when my family
would go to the beach,

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:46.645
I would go for a hike,
which is boring.

00:05:46.645 --> 00:05:48.748
But I figured out
how to make it fun.

00:05:48.748 --> 00:05:52.351
Whenever I go on a hike,
I always bring a ketchup packet,

00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:55.321
and halfway through the hike,
I open it up and I rub it

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:56.989
on my hands and my face,

00:05:56.989 --> 00:06:01.127
and then I just approach
romantic couples and ask,

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:02.795
you know,
"Have you seen my wife?"

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:05.831
[ Laughter ]
And they're like, "Uh, no."

00:06:05.831 --> 00:06:08.100
And I'm like, "But you heard her
sassing off to me."

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.902
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:12.371
It's like,
"I can still smell her scent."

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:14.607
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:16.175
That's inappropriate.
-That's a good transition

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:18.043
to the fact that your show
is called "The Fun Tour."

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:19.579
-It is fun.
-It is fun.

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:22.181
-We deserve fun.
-We do deserve fun right now.

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:25.851
I feel like -- I don't know --
I feel like people are happy

00:06:25.851 --> 00:06:27.687
to get back out and do comedy.

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:29.155
But I know a lot of people,

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:31.557
justifiably so as well,
wearing masks.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:33.959
What's it like to do comedy
to the masked?

00:06:33.959 --> 00:06:36.328
-Well, it's great
to have another barrier

00:06:36.328 --> 00:06:37.829
from hearing laughter.
-Yes.

00:06:37.829 --> 00:06:39.498
-Right? But no.

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:42.634
It is -- There is -- I mean,
I did the drive-in shows,

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:45.071
which were horrible.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:46.439
-This is people, like,
honking for laughs?

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:49.775
-Yeah. That's when you're
trying to, like, you know,

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:51.310
someone flashes their lights.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.414
You're essentially trying to
make the person

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:56.715
that is the most sober
in the car laugh,

00:06:56.715 --> 00:06:58.483
because they control things.
-Yeah.

00:06:58.483 --> 00:07:00.219
It's interesting, so you're --
I'm assuming you were

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:03.222
trying new material and your
feedback is lights and horns.

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:05.991
Could you process "this is
working, this isn't working"?

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:08.227
-Kind of.
Sometimes people would tailgate,

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:09.828
but it was just very weird.
-Yeah.

00:07:09.828 --> 00:07:13.132
-But, by the way, at Radio City,
they have --

00:07:13.132 --> 00:07:15.067
you know, everyone has to be
vaxxed so people don't --

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:16.835
If you're vaccinated,
you know --

00:07:16.835 --> 00:07:19.772
If you're under 12,
you have to wear a mask.

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:24.143
So, there are -- It's -- And
there is an energy, as we know.

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:26.279
It's like people
want to be entertained.

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.346
-Yes.

00:07:27.346 --> 00:07:29.348
-People are sick of
their families.

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:31.283
-Very sick.
-And so they need a break.

00:07:31.283 --> 00:07:33.686
-And yet, they -- I think they
enjoy listening to someone

00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:36.255
like you tell jokes about how
you're sick of your family.

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:37.456
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:39.892
I think we all need,
you know, some therapy.

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:41.460
Right?
-That's a very universal thing

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:42.862
that you are very good
at sharing.

00:07:42.862 --> 00:07:43.896
-Oh, thanks.

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:45.331
-It's always a delight
to see you.

00:07:45.331 --> 00:07:47.032
Have great shows over there.
-Thanks so much for having me.

00:07:47.032 --> 00:07:49.735
-For more information on tour
dates, go to jimgaffigan.com.

